1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:16,857
Welcome to Cybersecurity Basics. Most attacks don't begin with brilliant hacking. They begin with a reused password, a rushed click, or a missed update. Over the next few minutes, we'll walk through thirteen areas, and the simple habits in each one that keep you, and your organization, safe.

2
00:00:18,357 --> 00:00:22,676
Let's start with passwords and accounts. Your passwords are the keys to everything else.

3
00:00:23,475 --> 00:00:28,445
Use a unique, long passphrase for every account. Aim for sixteen characters or more.

4
00:00:29,195 --> 00:00:34,779
Store your passwords in a reputable password manager, instead of reusing them or writing them down.

5
00:00:35,529 --> 00:00:40,823
Turn on multi-factor authentication, especially for email, banking, and admin accounts.

6
00:00:41,573 --> 00:00:47,541
Where you can, choose passkeys or hardware security keys over text message codes, which can be intercepted.

7
00:00:48,291 --> 00:00:53,027
And watch for breach alerts. If a password shows up in a leak, change it right away.

8
00:00:54,527 --> 00:01:00,518
Next, phishing and social engineering. Attackers know it's often easier to trick a person than to break a system.

9
00:01:01,318 --> 00:01:07,657
Be suspicious of urgent requests for money, credentials, or gift cards. Pressure is the attacker's favorite tool.

10
00:01:08,407 --> 00:01:12,285
Check the actual sender address, and hover over links before you click them.

11
00:01:13,035 --> 00:01:17,551
Go to websites directly, rather than through links in unexpected emails or texts.

12
00:01:18,301 --> 00:01:24,245
Verify unusual requests by calling back on a number you already know, never one that's in the message.

13
00:01:24,995 --> 00:01:30,533
And report suspicious messages to IT, or to your email provider. Your report could protect everyone else.

14
00:01:32,033 --> 00:01:34,204
Now, your devices and software.

15
00:01:35,004 --> 00:01:40,809
Install security updates promptly, especially for browsers, operating systems, and V P Ns.

16
00:01:41,559 --> 00:01:46,424
Run antivirus, or endpoint detection and response software, on every device.

17
00:01:47,174 --> 00:01:51,725
Enable full disk encryption. That's Bit Locker on Windows, and File Vault on Mac.

18
00:01:52,475 --> 00:01:57,258
Set devices to lock automatically, and require a pin or a biometric to get back in.

19
00:01:58,008 --> 00:02:02,339
And remove software you no longer use. Every extra program is one more way in.

20
00:02:03,839 --> 00:02:04,710
On to networks.

21
00:02:05,510 --> 00:02:09,503
Change the default passwords on your router, and on every connected device.

22
00:02:10,253 --> 00:02:17,556
Use W P A three, or W P A two, on your Wi-Fi, and keep guest networks separate from business systems.

23
00:02:18,306 --> 00:02:21,394
Use a firewall, and close any ports you don't need.

24
00:02:22,144 --> 00:02:29,191
On public Wi-Fi, use a V P N, or a zero trust access tool in place of a traditional V P N.

25
00:02:29,941 --> 00:02:33,784
And segment your network, so that one compromised device can't reach everything.

26
00:02:35,284 --> 00:02:39,116
Email security. Email is still the number one way attackers get in.

27
00:02:39,916 --> 00:02:47,137
Set up S P F, dee kim, and dee mark for your domain. Together, they stop other people from spoofing your email address.

28
00:02:47,887 --> 00:02:52,415
Enable spam and malware filtering, and sandboxing of attachments where it's available.

29
00:02:53,165 --> 00:02:58,738
And disable any automatic forwarding rules you didn't create. Attackers often add them to quietly read your mail.

30
00:03:00,238 --> 00:03:03,755
Data protection and backups. This is what saves you on your worst day.

31
00:03:04,555 --> 00:03:11,928
Follow the three, two, one rule. Three copies of your data, on two types of media, with one copy off site or offline.

32
00:03:12,678 --> 00:03:16,439
Test your restores regularly. An untested backup is not a backup.

33
00:03:17,189 --> 00:03:21,961
Keep at least one backup offline, or immutable, to protect against ransomware.

34
00:03:22,711 --> 00:03:25,881
Encrypt sensitive data, both at rest and in transit.

35
00:03:26,630 --> 00:03:30,880
And limit who can access sensitive data. When people leave, remove their access.

36
00:03:32,380 --> 00:03:33,332
Access control.

37
00:03:34,132 --> 00:03:38,822
Apply least privilege. Give each person only the access their job requires.

38
00:03:39,572 --> 00:03:44,553
Use separate admin accounts for administrative tasks, not for daily email and browsing.

39
00:03:45,303 --> 00:03:48,693
Review user access, and shared accounts, on a regular schedule.

40
00:03:49,443 --> 00:03:52,392
And remove a former employee's access the same day they leave.

41
00:03:53,892 --> 00:03:58,675
Ransomware and incident response. When something goes wrong, the first few minutes matter most.

42
00:03:59,475 --> 00:04:03,736
Keep an incident response plan, with clear contacts, and clear steps.

43
00:04:04,486 --> 00:04:10,651
Isolate infected machines right away. Disconnect them from the network, but don't power them off. That preserves evidence.

44
00:04:11,401 --> 00:04:17,008
Report incidents to your IT provider, your legal counsel, and, where required, to regulators.

45
00:04:17,758 --> 00:04:21,416
And keep logs for your key systems, so you can investigate what happened.

46
00:04:22,916 --> 00:04:25,110
Cloud, and software as a service.

47
00:04:25,910 --> 00:04:33,955
Enable multi-factor authentication, and audit logging, on Microsoft three sixty five, Google Workspace, and your other cloud platforms.

48
00:04:34,705 --> 00:04:38,374
Review your sharing settings, to prevent public links to sensitive files.

49
00:04:39,124 --> 00:04:44,523
Monitor for unusual log-ins, like a new country, or impossible travel between two places.

50
00:04:45,273 --> 00:04:50,242
And check the security settings of third party apps that have access to your email, or your files.

51
00:04:51,742 --> 00:04:56,305
Vendors and the supply chain. Your security is only as strong as the companies you trust.

52
00:04:57,105 --> 00:05:00,251
Vet a vendor's security practices before you share data with them.

53
00:05:01,001 --> 00:05:03,334
Limit third party access, and monitor it.

54
00:05:04,084 --> 00:05:09,808
And watch for compromised software updates, and unexpected changes, even from suppliers you trust.

55
00:05:11,308 --> 00:05:12,330
Mobile devices.

56
00:05:13,130 --> 00:05:17,843
Install apps only from official stores, and review the permissions each app asks for.

57
00:05:18,593 --> 00:05:22,251
Keep your phone updated, and avoid jail breaking or rooting it.

58
00:05:23,001 --> 00:05:27,389
And where possible, use separate work and personal profiles, or containers.

59
00:05:28,889 --> 00:05:33,777
Human factors. Technology alone is never enough. People are the strongest defense.

60
00:05:34,577 --> 00:05:38,385
Run regular security awareness training, with simulated phishing tests.

61
00:05:39,135 --> 00:05:44,325
Make reporting mistakes safe and easy, so people report quickly, instead of hiding errors.

62
00:05:45,075 --> 00:05:50,717
And create a culture where asking for verification is normal, even when the request comes from an executive.

63
00:05:52,217 --> 00:05:59,055
Finally, AI and emerging threats. Artificial intelligence is changing the game, for attackers and defenders alike.

64
00:05:59,855 --> 00:06:09,190
AI generated phishing. Attackers can now write convincing, error free messages at scale, so good grammar is no longer a reliable sign that a message is safe.

65
00:06:09,940 --> 00:06:16,673
Deep fakes. Voices and video can be faked, so verify any payment or account change through a second, known channel.

66
00:06:17,423 --> 00:06:27,965
Prompt injection. Malicious instructions hidden in emails, web pages, or documents can manipulate the AI tools that read them. So limit what those tools are allowed to do automatically.

67
00:06:28,715 --> 00:06:37,040
Data leakage. Don't paste confidential client data, credentials, or proprietary code into public AI services, unless your organization has approved them.

68
00:06:37,790 --> 00:06:45,940
Shadow AI Employees using unapproved AI apps can expose data. So set a clear AI use policy, and provide approved tools.

69
00:06:46,690 --> 00:06:54,283
AI in defense. It helps with threat detection, log analysis, and alert triage. But it should supplement human review, not replace it.

70
00:06:55,033 --> 00:07:02,753
And verify AI output. It can make mistakes, or invent facts, including code with vulnerabilities. So review it before you rely on it.

71
00:07:04,253 --> 00:07:22,388
That's the basics. Remember, security isn't something you buy once. It's a set of small habits. Use strong, unique passwords with multi-factor authentication. Pause, and verify, before you click or pay. Keep your systems updated and backed up. And if something looks wrong, report it right away. Thanks for watching, and stay safe.
